<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Patient app backup / device-transfer rules (re-audit lane L, finding
  "PHI survives a phone-to-phone transfer").

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  The manifest previously declared only android:allowBackup="false". On
  Android 12 (API 31) Google split the single "backup" concept into two
  independent channels, and allowBackup only governs one of them:

    <cloud-backup>    Auto Backup to the user's Google Drive, and `adb backup`.
                      Suppressed by android:allowBackup="false" on every API
                      level, so this block is belt-and-braces.
    <device-transfer> The phone-to-phone (D2D) migration path used by the
                      Android setup wizard and by OEM tools built on it
                      (Samsung Smart Switch, Pixel's "Copy apps & data").
                      android:allowBackup="false" does NOT suppress it — a
                      new handset restored from the patient's old one would
                      arrive with the whole app data dir, including the
                      offline PHI cache and the EncryptedSharedPreferences
                      blob behind flutter_secure_storage.

  This app is a consumer-device patient app holding clinical records, so both
  channels exclude EVERYTHING. `exclude` with no matching `include` is a
  full-sandbox exclusion; there is deliberately no <include> anywhere below.

  DOMAIN COVERAGE
  The nine values below are the complete set the platform accepts for a
  `domain` attribute: AOSP maps exactly these nine strings to a backup tree
  token (FullBackup.BackupScheme.getTokenForXmlDomain), and Android Lint's
  FullBackupContentDetector validates against the same nine. Anything else is
  skipped at run time with only a verbose log, so a misspelled domain reads
  as "excluded" while the data it names is still copied — which is what the
  "known domains" assertion in the guard test exists to catch.

  Every domain needs its own line, and none of them is covered by "root". A
  backup walks each domain from that domain's own directory and prunes an
  entry only on an EXACT canonical-path match (BackupAgent.fullBackupFileTree
  via manifestExcludesContainFilePath), while path="." canonicalises to the
  domain directory itself — so one exclusion prunes exactly one walk, at its
  root. The files/, databases/ and shared_prefs/ walks each start below the
  data dir and are untouched by the "root" line above them. "Exclude all
  nine" is what makes this "exclude the app sandbox".

    root              /data/user/0/<pkg>/ itself, minus the files/,
                      databases/ and shared_prefs/ subtrees the framework
                      holds out of this walk for their own domains. This is
                      where app_flutter/ lives — path_provider's application
                      documents dir (offline API cache, downloaded reports),
                      because Flutter's PathUtils resolves it to
                      getDir("flutter") on the data dir.
    file              .../files/
    database          .../databases/
    sharedpref        .../shared_prefs/ — shared_preferences, and
                      flutter_secure_storage's EncryptedSharedPreferences.
    external          Android/data/<pkg>/ on shared storage — the FileProvider
                      external-files Download dir.
    device_root       The device-protected ("Direct Boot") twins of the four
    device_file       app-internal domains above, under /data/user_de/. Do
    device_database   not assume these are empty: Firebase's init provider and
    device_sharedpref messaging service merge into this app as
                      directBootAware components. Excluding them keeps
                      "everything is excluded" a property of this file rather
                      than of which storage context each dependency picks.

  Not listed because they need no line: cache/, code_cache/ and no_backup/
  (and their device-protected twins) are never part of a backup set and have
  no domain token to name them by.

  WHAT THIS DOES **NOT** COVER — do not read this file as "PHI cannot leave
  the device":
    • Rooted / bootloader-unlocked handsets, or a full filesystem image. The
      backup manifest is an instruction to the OS, not encryption at rest.
    • Data outside our sandbox: Health Connect records (owned by
      com.google.android.apps.healthdata), system notification history,
      screenshots, and anything the patient shares out via the OS share sheet.
    • iOS. There is no equivalent iOS backup exclusion in this repo yet — see
      docs/ROADMAP.md ("iOS has no backup exclusion at all").
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="external" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" path="." />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="external" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" path="." />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
